WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Meet Allocator Memo Builder.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.000
It turns a messy fund universe into an investment committee memo.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.000
And every claim in that memo can be checked.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
An allocator gets a messy manager universe, and a mandate.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.000
The job is a memo the investment committee can defend.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.000
But where does each number in that memo come from?

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.000
So the work is split three ways.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Code computes.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.000
The model writes.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.000
And code checks what it wrote.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
The input is three CSVs: fund terms, plus monthly returns in long and wide format.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:43.000
The sample universe is synthetic: twelve invented funds, with twenty-two deliberate problems.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Name variants, and a typo.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.000
Returns in percent instead of decimal.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Missing months, an outlier, a duplicate, a short track record, and a fund with only summary stats.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.000
Every problem is documented, so the sample doubles as a test oracle.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.000
Load the bundled sample.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:09.000
The data quality page lists every fix, with the file, row and column it came from.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Each fix becomes evidence the memo can cite.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Exact name matches merge automatically, but fuzzy matches are only proposals.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Orrin Vale, missing an s, is the same fund, so it's accepted.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Saltmarsh Global Macro two is a different vehicle, so it's rejected.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Next, the mandate.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Quarterly liquidity, sixty days notice, a twelve-month lockup.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:42.000
An eight percent volatility target, a twenty percent drawdown limit, a two percent fee cap, and three years of track record.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Global macro preferred, no credit, a shortlist of three, against a sixty-forty blend.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Benchmarks come from FRED: a committed snapshot runs offline, and one button refreshes it live.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Return, volatility, Sharpe, drawdown, correlation and beta are all computed in code, and tested.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Peer correlations show how much the funds move together.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Screening is code too, and only the five funds that pass every hard filter reach the model.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:20.000
When the memo compares funds, it uses a common window, so each one is measured over the same months.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.000
The model never computes a number.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:31.000
Every fact it can use is an evidence record with a stable id: a source cell, a metric, a mandate field, a screening result.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:39.000
It answers through one strict tool, and every claim carries a value, a unit, and the ids behind it.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Now, a live run against Claude.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:47.000
The prompt is built from evidence only, and every claim is verified before the memo appears.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:56.000
The result: a ranked shortlist with rationale, then the memo, with a summary, a recommendation, key risks, and a data appendix.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.000
Every line carries a claim marker.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Verification is deterministic.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Each id must resolve, and each number is converted to the evidence's unit and compared within a tolerance.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Every claim gets one status: verified, mismatch, unsupported, or qualitative cited.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
If anything fails, the model gets one retry, with the list of failures.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:29.000
Wording counts too: a loss of eight point eight six percent, stated as a positive number, is signed by its wording before it's compared.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:35.000
So it matches the minus eight point eight six percent in the evidence, and verifies.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Flagged claims are never dropped.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.000
Click a marker to open the audit view.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:48.000
This claim says F04's maximum drawdown lasted 28 months, and it's verified against the computed metric.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:56.000
The trail shows the metric's window, formula and source rows, down to the CSV cell, with the delta and tolerance.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.000
The next claim is qualitative, and resolves to the notes cell it cites in funds.csv.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:07.000
This live run took two model calls, and left zero claims flagged.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:12.000
The memo and the full audit download as Markdown and JSON.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.000
Each design decision is recorded in an ADR.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.000
Streamlit, with no business logic in the UI.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.000
SQLite, and a JSON bundle per run.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.000
FRED, with a committed cache.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.000
One strict tool for the model.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.000
A deterministic verifier that flags, and never drops.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.000
And seeded sample data as a test oracle.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:41.000
Tests cover validation, metrics and verification, and CI is a test gate only.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.000
There are known limitations.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.000
The S and P series is price return, without dividends.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:56.000
FRED serves only about three years of corporate bond history, so longer windows use a Treasury proxy, which is an approximation.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:03.000
There's no PDF export, it's single-user and local, and live ranking isn't deterministic.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.000
But every run is checkable.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.000
Every number computed.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.000
Every claim checked.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.900
That's Allocator Memo Builder.

